+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Precision:** Floating-point errors in projection may cause blocks near the frustum boundary to be incorrectly culled or included.
+*   **Degenerate Input:** Blocks with zero height ($z_{max}$ equal to base height) may result in degenerate regions.
+*   **Threshold Tuning:** The distance threshold is critical. Too low, and fine LODs are loaded unnecessarily; too high, and visual quality degrades. [G]
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Progressive Multiscale Loading
-A rendering strategy where each frame picks the appropriate resolution level per volume region, aiming for approximately one voxel per screen pixel. Coarse data fills the view first, and finer data replaces it region-by-region as it arrives, producing a complete coarse view within seconds that sharpens in place. It differs from standard dynamic loading by allowing mixed resolutions within a single frame and ensuring every cell is drawn exactly once, either from its own chunk or one resident coarser ancestor, to prevent double integration.
-*   **When to choose:** When visualizing massive volumes (e.g., histotomography) where immediate coarse visibility is critical, and memory bandwidth allows for fetching specific coarser ancestors.
-*   **Key Parameters:** Target resolution is coarsened to stay under 8192 cells and 60% of system memory.
-*   **Link:** [Progressive Multiscale Loading](4578-progressive-multiscale-loading.md)
+### [Progressive Multiscale Loading](13806-progressive-multiscale-loading.md)
+Progressive Multiscale Loading is a rendering strategy where each frame selects the appropriate resolution level per volume region, aiming for approximately one voxel per screen pixel. Coarse data fills the view first, and finer data replaces it region-by-region as it arrives, producing a complete coarse view within seconds that sharpens in place. It sets itself apart from static LOD systems by dynamically determining the minimum resolution required per frame based on projected size, avoiding the storage overhead of precomputed full-resolution hierarchies for extremely large 3D volumes.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of blocks and LOD levels. Visibility checks are $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of blocks. LOD selection involves distance checks [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduced VRAM usage by only loading visible blocks [S1]. In progressive variants, memory is bounded by the target resolution plus a small fraction for coarser ancestors (e.g., under 1/7 of target voxels for 2x downsampling) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Significantly reduces VRAM usage for large scenes [S1]. Provides immediate coarse visibility in progressive variants [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires efficient projection and visibility checks [S1]. Threshold-based LOD selection may cause popping if thresholds are not well-tuned [S1].
-*   **When to use:** Large-scale scenes with limited VRAM [S1]. Massive volumes requiring rapid initial visibility [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Small scenes where all data fits in memory [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of blocks and LOD levels. Visibility checks are $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of blocks. LOD selection is $O(L)$ where $L$ is the number of LOD levels per block.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** VRAM usage is reduced by only loading visible blocks and appropriate LODs. The reduction factor depends on the scene density and camera position.
+*   **Strengths:** Significant VRAM savings for large scenes; adaptive quality based on distance.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires dynamic visibility computation; may cause popping if LOD transitions are not smooth.
+*   **When to use:** Large-scale scenes with limited VRAM; real-time rendering on web or mobile platforms.
+*   **When not to use:** Small scenes where VRAM is not a constraint; scenes where static LODs are precomputed and fit in memory.
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of real-time neural rendering of large-scale scenes on the web [S1]. Developed to handle the memory constraints of rendering massive 3D data sets in web browsers [S1]. Progressive multiscale loading was further developed in the context of neuroglancer for histotomography data [S2, unofficial].
+Originated in the context of real-time neural rendering of large-scale scenes on the web, specifically in the "City-on-Web" project [S1]. The method addresses the challenge of rendering massive urban environments with limited client-side resources by dynamically managing data loading based on visibility and distance [S1].
 
